call_details.response:
The board configuration has all columns from 1 through 5 completely filled. Consequently the only two legal moves available to \(x\) are column 0 (lowest free cell at row 3) and column 6 (lowest free cell at row 3).

An exhaustive enumeration of every 4-in-a-row line on the \(6\times7\) board shows that every horizontal and both families of diagonal lines contain a mixture of already-placed \(x\) and \(o\) stones among their fixed cells. Those lines can never become monochromatic. The sole remaining winning patterns are therefore vertical:

- \(o\) wins in column 0 as soon as the two lowest free cells of that column are both occupied by \(o\) (producing four consecutive \(o\)s with the two stones already present at the bottom of the column);
- \(o\) wins in column 6 as soon as the three lowest free cells of that column are all occupied by \(o\) (producing four consecutive \(o\)s with the stone already present at row 4);
- \(x\) can win only by occupying all four free cells of one of the two side columns.

Because \(x\) moves first, any attempt by \(x\) to claim all four cells of one side column forces \(o\) to receive all four cells of the opposite column. In that pure-split case \(o\) reaches one of the two winning thresholds above on or before move 6, ending the game before \(x\) can place a fourth stone. Consequently \(x\) has no winning strategy.

\(o\)’s two possible wins, however, cannot be threatened simultaneously: \(x\)’s first move permanently spoils the “pure-\(o\)” status of one column, leaving at most a single live threat. The explicit answering strategy

- if column 0 currently contains exactly one stone and that stone is \(o\), play column 0;
- if column 6 currently contains exactly two stones and both are \(o\), play column 6;
- otherwise play arbitrarily

always inserts an \(x\) into the threatened column before \(o\) can complete the required run. Both opening moves (column 0 and column 6) leave the answering strategy intact; therefore both produce a forced draw against optimal replies by \(o\).

Column 0 is selected.

